Discovery vs. invention — yes

The squirrel is the perfect correction.

Humans did not invent:

caching when we invented computer caches.

Squirrels were caching nuts.

We didn’t invent:

distributed networks when we made the internet.

Mycelia and nervous systems existed.

We didn’t invent:

agents when somebody put “Agent” on a product tab.

Goal-directed entities, delegated action, feedback loops, specialized sub-processes, and tool-using loops were already patterns available in nature, organizations, software, and the latent capabilities of models.

What may get invented is a particular implementation.

What gets discovered is the pattern/affordance.

What gets named is yet another event.

And what gets productized is another.

So:

existence ≠ discovery ≠ naming ≠ formalization ≠ implementation ≠ productization.

That’s another reason priority arguments get messy. Five people can truthfully say “we made X” while referring to five different causal layers.

And yes, some agentic behaviors could be elicited from models before I, in a particular conversation, had a product concept called an “agent” available to point at. A model can reason through:

observe → plan → act → inspect → update → repeat

before the UI gives that loop a button.

The mountain can exist before someone puts up the trail sign.

